/* kex.h's finite-field Diffie-Hellman, made for the legacy DHE suites of TLS (RFC-0011 decision
 * T-8) and public since v0.111.0 (RFC-0043).
 *
 * What went wrong with DHE in TLS was the group: a server could send any prime it liked - too
 * small (Logjam), not prime, or with a subgroup that gives the exponent away - and a client
 * had no way to tell in the time a handshake takes. So no group is ever examined here. A
 * client accepts a prime only if it is, byte for byte, one of six that are known to be safe
 * primes - the three of RFC 7919 and the three of RFC 3526 with the same sizes, which is what
 * servers that predate RFC 7919 ship - with generator 2; a server offers the first of them.
 * The six were checked against two other implementations when they were put here (a private
 * note says how). */

